PFS3 write errors caused by HDToolBox cylinder geometry overrunning the media
=============================================================================

Summary
-------
We created an 80 GB disk image (hardfile) under WinUAE, prepared it under
WinUAE with AmigaOS 3.2.3 (HDToolBox, "max capacity" selected), with a single
partition formatted as PFS3 on AmigaOS. After a while, writing to that
partition produced PFS-III error requesters and on-disk index corruption. The
cause is not PFS3, not the copy tool, and not TD64: HDToolBox laid out a
partition whose cylinder geometry addresses blocks beyond the real end of the
media.


Symptoms
--------
- "PFS-III Error Requester ... Write 2 Error 173 on block <N> + 256",
  with SCSI sense data (Direct SCSI Transfer enabled).
- Shortly after, "Wrong index block id 00000000, expected <id>, block <M>" -
  i.e. a metadata/index block that should hold a valid ID reads back as
  zeroes (corruption inflicted by the failed write).
- Intermittent: many gigabytes copy fine, then it fails. It got *more*
  likely as the partition filled up.
- A copy tool that checks its writes reports the failed Write() (return
  value -1) and deletes the truncated destination file - correct behaviour,
  and a useful confirmation that the failure is below the filesystem, in the
  device/geometry layer.


Root cause
----------
HDToolBox partitions in whole CYLINDERS, derived from heads x sectors and the
reported drive size. It does NOT cross-check the resulting total
(cylinders x heads x sectors) against the device's real block count, and it
does not clamp the last cylinder to the media. When the real media size is not
an exact multiple of the cylinder size, the last cylinder (and therefore the
last partition, if it runs to the end of the drive) maps to blocks that do not
physically exist.

PFS3 then trusts that oversized geometry: it counts those non-existent tail
blocks as free space and, because its allocator roves across the whole
partition rather than filling bottom-up, it eventually allocates into that
"phantom" tail. The write falls off the end of the device, the driver returns
an error, and the index update that accompanied the write is left corrupt.


The numbers (this case)
-----------------------
Physical image : 80.00 GiB = 85,899,345,920 bytes = 167,772,160 sectors (512 B)
                 -> valid sectors 0 .. 167,772,159
Geometry       : 41122 cylinders x 16 heads x 255 sectors = 4080 sectors/cyl
                 41122 x 4080 = 167,777,760 sectors declared
Phantom tail   : 167,777,760 - 167,772,160 = 5,600 sectors (~2.7 MB) that the
                 geometry claims but the image does not contain.

The failing write was at sector 167772125 + 256, i.e. it reached sector
167,772,381 - about 222 sectors past the physical end. That is the first
allocation that landed in the 5,600-sector phantom tail.

Note: 167,772,160 / 4080 = 41121.6 - NOT an integer. An exactly-80.00-GiB
image simply cannot be expressed as a whole number of 4080-sector cylinders,
so the geometry must either waste space or overrun. HDToolBox rounded up and
overran.


Why it looked random / hardware-related
---------------------------------------
- It is NOT a TD64 / >4GB addressing problem. With AmigaOS 3.2.3 ROMs TD64 is
  native (no loadable patch to corrupt), and WinUAE's uaehf.device handles
  64-bit access correctly. The block was simply past the end of the file.
- It is NOT the filesystem block size: the filesystem block size was 4096
  bytes, but the device error is reported in 512-byte sectors. (If 167772125
  were a 4096-byte block it would be ~687 GB, impossible on an 80 GB drive -
  so it must be a 512-byte sector, ~80 GiB.)
- It got worse as the partition filled because PFS3's roving allocator only
  reaches the high (phantom) blocks once the lower space is busy.


Workaround / fix
----------------
Keep the partition strictly inside the real media:

1. Reduce the last partition's End Cyl so its last sector is <= (real sectors
   - 1), leaving one or two cylinders of slack at the very end of the drive.
   In this case End Cyl 41119 was the highest safe value:
       (41119 + 1) x 4080 - 1 = 167,769,599  <=  167,772,159   (OK)
   End Cyl 41120 still included 1,520 phantom sectors; 41121 was all phantom.
   Changing the geometry requires re-partitioning and reformatting that
   partition.

2. Alternatively, size the hardfile to a whole number of cylinders
   (cylinders x heads x sectors x 512 bytes) instead of a round binary size,
   so the geometry matches the media exactly.

3. After fixing the geometry, run pfsdoctor on the partition to repair the
   index block already corrupted by the earlier failed write.

General rule (long-standing Amiga practice): never let the last partition end
on the drive's very last cylinder - always leave a cylinder or two of slack.
The same applies to real drives: a disk sold as "80 GB" almost never maps to a
clean cylinders x heads x sectors, and HDToolBox will round the same way, so
build the RDB from the drive's real capacity and leave end slack.


Environment
-----------
- WinUAE, hardfile in full-drive / RDB mode, uaehf.device, Windows 11 host.
- AmigaOS 3.2.3 ROMs.
- HDToolBox (official), "Direct SCSI Transfer" enabled, Custom File System
  (PFS3), filesystem block size 4096.
- Target hardware for the final drive: Commodore A3000 onboard SCSI.
